---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import { REQUIREMENTS, SOURCE_URL } from "../data/site";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const permissions = [
  {
    name: "Camera",
    why: "To follow your eyes. The video is processed on the Mac and thrown away; it's never stored or sent anywhere.",
    where: "Privacy & Security → Camera",
  },
  {
    name: "Accessibility",
    why: "To find the buttons in other apps and press them for you when you look at them. This is what makes eye control work in every app.",
    where: "Privacy & Security → Accessibility",
  },
  {
    name: "Screen Recording",
    why: "So the AI agent can see the app it's working in, only during a task you started. Screenshots aren't kept by default.",
    where:
      "Privacy & Security → Screen & System Audio Recording (called Screen Recording on macOS 13 and 14)",
  },
  {
    name: "Microphone",
    why: "For phone calls through Talk, so AGENCY can hear the other person. Optional.",
    where: "Privacy & Security → Microphone",
  },
  {
    name: "Speech Recognition",
    why: "To turn what the other person says on a call into text you can read. Optional.",
    where: "Privacy & Security → Speech Recognition",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Setting up AGENCY"
  description="Install AGENCY on a Mac and give it the permissions it needs: camera, Accessibility, Screen Recording, and optionally the microphone and speech recognition."
  current="setup"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Setup guide"
    title="Setting up AGENCY"
    lede="For whoever sets AGENCY up, often a family member or caregiver with a mouse and keyboard. It takes about fifteen minutes. AGENCY's own onboarding walks through the same steps, and the person can carry on by eye once the camera works."
    tone="teal"
  />
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="note">
      The first Mac release is on its way. This guide describes setup as it's designed, and will be checked
      step by step against the first release. You can <a href={`${base}app/`}>try AGENCY in your browser</a>{" "}
      today.
    </p>

    <h2 id="before">1. Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>{REQUIREMENTS.macos}.</li>
      <li>{REQUIREMENTS.chips}.</li>
      <li>{REQUIREMENTS.camera}. Put the Mac at eye level, with light in front of the person, not behind.</li>
      <li>{REQUIREMENTS.browser}.</li>
      <li>The Mac's administrator password, to approve the permissions below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="install">2. Install</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <a href={`${base}download/`}>Download AGENCY</a> for this Mac.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open the downloaded .dmg file and drag <strong>AGENCY</strong> into <strong>Applications</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open AGENCY from Applications. If macOS says it can't open it, follow{" "}
        <a href={`${base}download/#unsigned`}>the steps for unsigned builds</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="permissions">3. Give AGENCY its permissions</h2>
    <p>
      macOS asks the first time AGENCY needs each one. You can also turn them on yourself: open{" "}
      <strong>System Settings</strong>, choose <strong>Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, open the section
      below, and switch <strong>AGENCY</strong> on. Some need AGENCY to be quit and reopened afterwards;
      AGENCY tells you when.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Permissions" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Permission</th>
            <th scope="col">Why AGENCY needs it</th>
            <th scope="col">Where to turn it on</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {permissions.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{p.name}</th>
              <td>{p.why}</td>
              <td>{p.where}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>Updating an unsigned build?</strong> macOS forgets the Accessibility and Screen Recording
      permissions every time an unsigned AGENCY is updated. Afterwards, open those two sections again, select
      AGENCY, remove it with the minus button, and add it again with plus (or switch it off and on). Signed
      builds keep their permissions.
    </p>

    <h2 id="eyes">4. Set up the eyes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>AGENCY checks the camera: face, eyes, light and distance, and says plainly what to change.</li>
      <li>
        Calibration: the person looks at a few dots in turn. AGENCY then measures how accurate it is on dots
        it didn't learn from, and says whether the result is good, fair or poor.
      </li>
      <li>
        If it's poor, try better light or a different position, or use a single switch, a head pointer, or the
        long-blink switch instead. Every screen works with all of them.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="browser">5. Eye control on web pages (Chrome)</h2>
    <p>
      To use web pages by eye, AGENCY installs a small Chrome extension called AGENCY Bridge. AGENCY's
      onboarding shows how to add it, and Chrome asks you to confirm. It only talks to AGENCY on the same Mac.
    </p>

    <h2 id="lifeline">6. Lifeline</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add the people to contact, in order, and how to reach them.</li>
      <li>
        Fill in the responder card: how the person communicates, breathing equipment to check first, how to
        get in.
      </li>
      <li>
        Give permission, once, for AGENCY to contact them without asking during an emergency. Without it,
        Lifeline only sounds the alarm on the Mac, and says so.
      </li>
      <li>Send a test alert, and practise the help signal (three long blinks) until it works reliably.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note note-lifeline">
      Out of the box, Lifeline contacts are simulated phones: no real person is messaged. Real text messages
      and calls need a Twilio account and real numbers to be set up deliberately. Lifeline is not an emergency
      service and never calls 911 by itself. How it works and what has been tested:{" "}
      <a href={`${SOURCE_URL}/blob/main/LIFELINE.md`}>LIFELINE.md</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ai">7. The AI agent (optional)</h2>
    <p>
      {REQUIREMENTS.ai} The key is kept in the Mac's keychain. AI tasks send the model only what the task
      needs, and Google's terms apply to it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a class="btn btn-sun" href={`${base}download/`}>
        Go to the download
      </a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
